course: english
lesson: 23
title: Reading non-fiction: recount, instructions, report
level: primary-4
stars: 0
explain: Non-fiction texts are about real things, and each kind has its own shape. A recount retells real events in time order: «On Tuesday, Class 4 went to the seaside. First, we found crabs in rock pools. Next, we ate lunch on the beach. Finally, we built a huge sandcastle.» Time words like first, next, then and finally show the order.
explain: Instructions tell you how to do something. «How to plant a seed. You will need: a pot, soil, a seed, water. 1. Fill the pot with soil. 2. Push the seed in. 3. Water it.» They have a title, a list of what you need, numbered steps in order, and bossy verbs (fill, push, water) at the start. Kate in Leeds says the instructions are «mardy» (northern for grumpy) because they boss you about, but bossy verbs are not rude: they make the job clear.
explain: A report, sometimes called a non-chronological report, gives facts about a topic grouped by idea, not by time: «Hedgehogs are small mammals covered in spines. They eat insects, worms and slugs. In winter, they hibernate.» Reports often have headings, pictures with captions, and a glossary at the end that explains the bold words. Ali skips the glossary and then gets stuck on «hibernate»: it means a long winter sleep.
explain: Dad builds a bookcase without reading the instructions, and it wobbles. Reading every step before you start saves trouble. And reports should be facts, things that can be checked, not opinions, which are what someone thinks. «Barn owls have heart-shaped faces» is a fact; «owls are the best birds» is an opinion.
fact: A recount retells real events in the order they happened.
fact: Recounts use time words such as first, next, then, after that and finally.
fact: A recount written by someone who was there uses «I» and «we»: this is first person. A recount about other people uses he, she, they or a name, like «Jo visited Gran»: this is third person.
fact: Instructions have a title, a list of what you need, numbered steps and bossy verbs.
fact: Bossy verbs, also called imperative verbs, tell you what to do: mix, fill, push, stir.
fact: Steps in instructions are numbered so you do them in the right order.
fact: A report gives facts about a topic, grouped by idea rather than by time.
fact: Even a dull report can teach you something: look for one surprising fact.
fact: A glossary is a list of key words and their meanings, usually at the back of a non-fiction book.
fact: A fact can be checked and proved; an opinion is what someone thinks or feels.
fact: Hedgehogs are small mammals with spines that eat insects, worms and slugs and hibernate in winter.
fact: Most owls hunt at night, and barn owls have heart-shaped faces.
fact: Slang: «mardy» (North and Midlands of England) means grumpy or sulky; «heaps» (Australia) can mean very or a lot.
exercise: In the seaside recount, what did Class 4 do first? | Found crabs in rock pools | Ate their lunch on the sandy beach | Built a sandcastle | Went home on the coach
exercise: Why are the steps in the seed instructions numbered? | So you do them in the right order | To make the page look neat and tidy for the teacher | To count the seeds | To show how long each step takes
exercise: In the hedgehog report, what do hedgehogs eat? | Insects, worms and slugs | Only fruit and nuts | Grass, leaves, flowers and any bread people throw out | Fish
exercise: Dad builds furniture without reading the instructions, and it wobbles. What is the lesson? | Read all the steps before you start | Instructions are always wrong | Furniture always wobbles a bit, so there is nothing to worry about | Use more glue
exercise: Kate says the instructions are mardy. What does mardy mean? | Grumpy | Very clever | Old | Kind
exercise: Why do instructions use bossy verbs like fill and push? | To tell you clearly what to do | Because the writer is rude and cross with the reader | To make them rhyme | To show they are a story
exercise: Ali skips the glossary at the end of a report. What does a glossary do? | Explains key words | Lists the pages | Shows the pictures again in a bigger size so you can see more | Tells the story
exercise: «Yesterday I visited Gran. We baked scones and played cards. I won!» Is this first or third person? | First person | Third person, because it is about a grandmother | Second person | No person
exercise: Ruby in Sydney says the koala report is «heaps boring». What does heaps mean here? | Very | Not at all | A little bit | Piles of leaves
exercise: «Mix the flour and eggs. Bake for 20 minutes.» What should you do after mixing? | Bake for 20 minutes | Mix again for 20 minutes | Eat the mixture | Add more eggs and then leave it to stand for an hour
exercise: «Most owls hunt at night. Barn owls have heart-shaped faces.» Fact or opinion? | Facts | Opinions | One fact and one opinion | Neither, they are questions
exercise: Which is an opinion? | Owls are the best birds | Barn owls have heart-shaped faces | Most owls hunt at night | Owls are birds that have feathers and lay eggs
exercise: How is a report organised? | By idea, not by time | By time, from the oldest to the newest event | Alphabetically by every word | It has no order
exercise: Which words show order in a recount? | First, next, finally | Big, small, round | Because, so, if | Fill, push, water
exercise: What does hibernate mean? | Have a long winter sleep | Hide from foxes | Eat a lot | Build a nest of leaves and twigs for the babies in spring
test: «a recount is just a story innit». What's the difference? | A recount retells real events; a story can be made up | There's no difference at all | A recount is always made up | A recount has no order
test: ★★ Which sentence belongs in instructions, not a recount? | Stir the paint until it is smooth | We stirred the paint until it was smooth | Next, Tom stirred the paint | Yesterday the paint was smooth
test: ★★ Ruby says the koala report is heaps boring. Mum: «What did you learn?» What is a good thing to do with a dull report? | Find one surprising fact | Stop reading at once | Decide that boring texts can't teach you anything | Only look at the pictures
test: ★★★ Instructions: «1. Crack the egg. 2. Whisk it. 3. Pour it into the hot pan.» Sam whisks first, then cracks. What will happen? | He'll whisk an egg still in its shell, so it won't work | It works just the same | The pan will cool down | The egg will crack itself
test: ★★★ «Last Saturday, we went to the zoo. The lions were asleep. Lions sleep up to 20 hours a day. Then we saw the penguins.» Which sentence is a report-style fact, not part of the recount of the day? | Lions sleep up to 20 hours a day | The lions were asleep | Then we saw the penguins | Last Saturday, we went to the zoo
test: In a cartoon, a character builds a rocket without instructions and it works first time. What happens in real life? | Skipping instructions usually leads to mistakes | It always works first time | Instructions slow you down | Rockets don't need instructions
test: «everyone knows hedgehogs are the cutest animal, it's a fact». Is it? | No, that's an opinion | Yes, everyone knows it | Yes, it's in a report | No, hedgehogs are not animals
test: What would happen if the hedgehog report had no headings and no glossary? | Facts and hard words would be harder to find | Hedgehogs would stop hibernating | It would be easier to read | Nothing at all
test: «Yesterday I visited Gran.» Change it to third person. | Yesterday Jo visited Gran. | Yesterday I visited my gran. | Tomorrow I'll visit Gran. | Visit Gran yesterday.
test: «she's mardy cos she lost the match». How is she feeling? | Grumpy and sulky | Proud and happy | Hungry | Scared of the dark
exam: Which of the following statements most accurately characterises a recount? | A retelling of actual events arranged in chronological order | A set of numbered directions | A collection of opinions | A fictional narrative concerning imaginary creatures in a faraway land
exam: Identify the principal function of the numbering of steps in a set of instructions. | To ensure that the steps are performed in the correct sequence | To decorate the page | To indicate the cost of each step | To enumerate the ingredients required for the recipe in order of their weight
exam: Select the response that accurately identifies a statement of opinion. | Owls are the finest of all birds | Barn owls possess heart-shaped faces | The majority of owls hunt nocturnally | Owls are birds, which is to say feathered creatures that lay eggs
exam: Ascertain the function of a glossary appended to a non-chronological report. | To define key terms used in the text | To list the chapters | To summarise the report's conclusions in the form of a single paragraph | To display photographs
